WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:05.790
This is the story of how we might discover life outside our solar system.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.390
Here is a star in our galaxy.

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:12.650
It&amp;#39;s a bit cooler than our sun, which means

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:15.560
it emits more red light and less blue light.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:20.615
It&amp;#39;s also a little smaller, which means it emits less energy.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:27.770
This orange star, therefore, has habitable zone that&amp;#39;s closer in than

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:28.600
our own sun&amp;#39;s.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:35.730
By luck, a planet forms in that habitable zone -- not too cold,

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:36.350
not too hot.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:42.195
The planet is a terrestrial world, composed of rock and metals,

00:00:42.195 --> 00:00:44.320
although at this time, the entire planet is molten.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.540
It&amp;#39;s large for a terrestrial world, heavier than our earth --

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:51.070
what we would call a super-Earth.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:57.560
Now, asteroids and comets bombard this super-Earth during its formation,

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.170
sometimes really violently.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:04.060
The surface remains molten for many, many years.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:08.060
The planet&amp;#39;s gravitational pull also captures a few asteroids,

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:09.305
which become small moons.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:14.350
As the planet cools, its outer layer becomes solid.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.463
This is the first crust for our super-Earth.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:23.950
Beneath the crust is a thick liquid mantle with convection currents.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:27.880
These start the process of plate tectonics.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:33.140
The planet&amp;#39;s crust will be moved and recycled over time.

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:38.534
The water and gases delivered by all the bombarding comets

00:01:38.534 --> 00:01:40.117
contribute to the planet&amp;#39;s atmosphere.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:45.210
The planet&amp;#39;s high mass and resulting gravitational pull

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.455
allows it to retain a fairly thick atmosphere.

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:52.950
This will be essential in protecting its surface from heavy radiation

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:56.970
from its star and will also help moderate the atmospheric temperature.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.790
As the planet cools even farther, atmospheric water vapor

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:04.480
becomes liquid and rains down onto the planet.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.585
Seas and lakes develop.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:13.640
Because the planet&amp;#39;s in its star&amp;#39;s habitable zone, most of that water

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:18.760
will remain liquid, though the planet may develop ice caps like our own,

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.250
or the ones on planet Mars.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:26.110
We now have a place of shelter where many elements mix together

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:27.400
in the warm, churning sea.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.780
This is the sort of environment where life can develop.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:37.056
Rains bring nutrients from the land onto the sea,

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:38.680
where they collect along the shoreline.

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:44.600
These mixing chemicals, with some energy from heat, light,

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:49.610
and electrical discharges, combine to form amino acids, lipids,

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:51.335
and other simple organic molecules.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:58.340
These in turn react with each other to produce longer and more complex

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:00.460
molecules, similar to RNA.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:07.560
These molecules both store information and catalyze their own creation,

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.840
making it easier to form more of this alien RNA.

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:15.005
The process actually helps itself continue.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.910
Now bubbles from the sea foam collect lipids,

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:24.340
forming a simple surface: the first prototypes of cell membranes.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:32.670
As time goes on, these bubbles fill with water, salt, and nutrients, as well as

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.290
some of this alien RNA.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:40.900
When these surfaces allow certain molecules to enter and others to leave,

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:42.690
we have the first true cell.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:48.670
The alien RNA provides genetic information and replication.

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:51.280
The nutrient-rich saltwater provides the cytoplasm.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.650
The bubble of lipids provides the cell membrane.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:59.890
So cells require genetic material, cytoplasm, and the membrane,

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:02.710
and this environment has created all three of them.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.575
At this point, evolution becomes possible.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:12.450
When the RNA replicates itself enough, it will split the bubble in two,

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.640
and cell division begins.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:18.649
The alien RNA will be changed by radiation or replication

00:04:18.649 --> 00:04:20.470
errors and other factors.

00:04:21.589 --> 00:04:25.476
In each generation of change, the cells are challenged by their environment.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:31.090
So cells with changes that don&amp;#39;t help them survive and reproduce

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:33.980
will be crowded out by the cells with changes

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:35.745
that do help them survive and replicate.

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:41.000
Cellular processes will become more complex and robust,

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.630
and this evolving life will be able to survive beyond the place

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:45.920
where it first arose.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:52.690
As the cells divide and produce, more and more of the planet

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:54.020
becomes a home to life.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:59.280
And life, even single celled life like this, produces byproducts.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.800
Modern plant life on Earth produces oxygen gas, but early life on Earth

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:12.300
found oxygen poisonous, so we know that not all life needs or produces oxygen.

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:17.640
Life on our hypothetical planet produces methane

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:20.840
as a byproduct, as well as nitrous oxide.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:29.440
These chemicals will be our clue that life is present on this super-Earth

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:32.895
Now, back on Earth, we are surveying the sky for new planets.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:38.980
At sometime in our future, our telescopes happen to view this star.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:45.570
The star brightness and color tell us about its size and its mass.

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:48.210
We see the star&amp;#39;s brightness dim periodically,

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:51.450
indicating that it may have a planet orbiting it

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:52.930
with a transit we can detect.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:59.310
Continued observation confirms this cyclical light pattern,

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:02.100
so we bring new instruments to focus on the star.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.480
Examining this light curve closely and using

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:11.130
what we know about the star&amp;#39;s own size, we can estimate the size of the planet.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:16.330
Now we bring a spectrometer to use and it

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:21.515
lets us see, very precisely, what colors of light are coming from the star.

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:27.310
These colors oscillate as the star moves towards us and away from us, due

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:28.606
to the Doppler shift effect.

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:34.440
Knowing how fast the star moves and how massive it is,

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.510
we determine the mass of the planet, as well as

00:06:37.510 --> 00:06:39.050
the radius of the planet&amp;#39;s orbit.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:44.260
From all this information, we on Earth find out

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:47.892
that the planet is within its star&amp;#39;s habitable zone

00:06:47.892 --> 00:06:49.100
and that it is a super-Earth.

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:52.495
Now we examine it more closely.

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:00.310
Very careful work with the spectrometer will tell us what colors of light

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:03.440
are coming from the planet, and which wavelengths

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:04.790
are blocked by its atmosphere.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:08.990
Most interesting to us are spectral lines

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:12.110
that would indicate the presence of volatiles gases

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:17.460
like oxygen gas, nitrous oxide, or methane in the planet&amp;#39;s atmosphere.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:24.220
The spectral lines from this planet show a great amount of methane gas--

00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:27.590
much more than could be explained by volcanoes

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:29.915
or other non-life-based sources.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:35.560
As we watch carefully over the course of several years,

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:38.770
we confirm the presence of methane, as well as

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:42.210
nitrous oxide, another marker of biological life.

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:47.205
The evidence is clear: we have found a planet with life!

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:52.610
Well, this is only one way the story might come out.

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:55.775
For now, we will need to keep watching, keep exploring.

00:07:56.810 --> 00:08:00.420
As new technologies and techniques improve our ability to view

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:04.790
and measured the universe, it&amp;#39;s only a matter of time.

